Absolutely. History may be one of the strongest uses because it naturally benefits from separating what is known, what is inferred, and what is simulated.

You could build a historical world-state from primary sources, archaeology, maps, census records, military logs, letters, economic data, climate records, material culture, and later scholarship. Then represent uncertainty explicitly instead of pretending the past is fully observed.

A useful stack would be:

\text{evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{historical state model}
\rightarrow
\text{agent/world simulation}
\rightarrow
\text{possible trajectories}
\rightarrow
\text{rendered perspective}

Then you can ask things like: “Show me this battle from the logistics officer’s perspective,” “What information did this ruler actually have at the time?”, “How long would this message have taken to travel?”, “What would daily life have looked like for a merchant here?”, or “Which assumptions are doing the most work in this reconstruction?”

That is much better than a static documentary because the system can expose the causal machinery.

The really powerful part is counterfactual study:

\text{same initial state}
\rightarrow
\text{change one variable}
\rightarrow
\text{observe divergence}

Remove a road. Delay a message. Change crop yields. Move an army two days later. Alter a succession. Then see which outcomes are robust and which depended on fragile contingencies.

But you’d want a strict provenance layer:

Known — directly supported by evidence.
Probable — supported by multiple sources or strong inference.
Plausible — consistent with evidence but uncertain.
Speculative — useful for exploration, not a historical claim.

That prevents the simulation from becoming a convincing fiction that quietly overwrites the record.

So yes: instead of merely “learning history,” you could enter a bounded model of history, inspect it from many viewpoints, stress-test causal claims, and see where uncertainty lives.

That turns history from a timeline into a navigable state-space.